      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Off-pump coronary artery bypass surgery has been demonstrated to reduce morbidity in elective patients. However, high-risk patients might benefit the most from this surgical procedure. Our goal was to investigate the effectiveness of on-pump and off-pump coronary artery bypass surgery on early clinical outcome in a consecutive series of overweight patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND RESULTS",
          "content": "From April 1996 to April 2001, data on 4321 patients undergoing coronary surgery (mortality 1.4%) were prospectively entered into the Patient Analysis and Tracking System. Data were extracted for all patients with a body mass index > or =25 kg/m(2). A risk-adjusted analysis was performed to assess the effect of surgical technique in the whole overweight cohort. 2844 patients were identified (2261 male, median age 63, interquartile range 56 to 68). Patients undergoing on-pump surgery (2170, 76.3%) were less likely than those undergoing off-pump surgery to have hypercholesterolemia or left main stem disease and were, on average, less obese. However, they were more likely to have unstable angina and to have had a previous myocardial infarction, and they had more extensive coronary disease and received more grafts (all P<0.05). Unadjusted analyses, taking account only of consultant team, showed significant benefits of off-pump surgery in terms of hospital deaths, arrhythmias, inotropic use, use of intra-aortic balloon pump, blood loss, transfusion requirement, postoperative hemoglobin, chest infections, neurological complications, intensive care unit and hospital stay (all P<0.05). After adjustment for confounding prognostic factors, the benefits of off-pump surgery were still significant for death in hospital, transfusion requirement, postoperative hemoglobin, neurological complications, intensive care unit and hospital stay (ORs 0.35 to 0.79, P<0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These results suggest that off-pump surgery is safe and effective and is associated with a reduced in-hospital mortality and morbidity in overweight patients when compared with conventional coronary surgery with cardiopulmonary bypass and cardioplegic arrest."
        }
      ]},
